b. The annual storm damage expense accrual;
c. actual storm damage charges to the reserve; and d. The resulting storm damage reserve ending balance at December 31, 2025.

According to the Company’s 2022 Selective Meter Test Report, with the exception of the AMI meter strata identified as “SEICNA1”, all of the other AMI meter strata identified in Table C of the 2022 Selective Meter Test Report are currently being installed.  Please provide the following information:
The Company’s plans for time-varying rates; and indicate whether any such offering would be the default tariff for customers with smart meters;
The Company’s proposals for any customer “opt-out” provisions;
The Company’s analysis of how the AMI plan promotes demand response, energy efficiency, and conservation.

response:
The start date was previously defined as “current Month – 13.” The pseudo code was as follows for both Android and iOS:
var startDate = DateTime.Today.AddMonths(-13)
setUsageSummary(billHistory) {
        let billCount = 1;
        let thisMonth = false;
        let previousMonth = false;
        let lastYear = false;
        for (const bill of billHistory) {
            if (bill.Description === 'Bill') {
                let billDate = new Date(bill.DateActivity);
                if (thisMonth === false) {
                    this.thisMonthBill = bill;
                }
                if (thisMonth === true && previousMonth === false) {
                    this.lastMonthBill = bill;
                    previousMonth = true;
                }
                thisMonth = true;
                if (billCount >= 13) {
                    this.lastYearBill = bill;
                    break;
                }
                billCount++;
            }
        }
    }
}

Start Date is now defined as: current Month – 14
The pseudo code is as follows for both Android and iOS:
var startDate = DateTime.Today.AddMonths(-14)
setUsageSummary(billHistory) {
         let billCount = 1;
         let thisMonth = false;
         let previousMonth = false;
         let lastYear = false;
         for (const bill of billHistory) {
             if (bill.Description === 'Bill') {
                 let billDate = new Date(bill.DateActivity);
                 if (thisMonth === false) {
                     this.thisMonthBill = bill;
                 }
                 if (thisMonth === true && previousMonth === false) {
                     this.lastMonthBill = bill;
                     previousMonth = true;
                 }
                 thisMonth = true;
                 if (billCount >= 14) {
                     this.lastYearBill = bill;
                     break;
                 }
                 billCount++;
             }
         }
     }
 }

The Company implemented a storm modeling tool, Storm Prediction, Estimated Restoration Times and Reporting (“SPEAR”), in July 2025. This storm analytics tool delivers:
Predicted incident counts;
Forecasted customer impacts;
Hourly timing estimates;
Geospatial impact forecasts, both at the state and region levels; and
Weather metric predictions.
As part of the SPEAR application, the Company also recently implemented a manpower tracking tool called GridCritical, which will be used to efficiently onboard, track, and accommodate resources assigned to restorations efforts.  This information will feed directly into the SPEAR application to assist in customer restoration times.
In addition to SPEAR, the Company is currently testing two additional modeling tools (StormImpact Prediction Models and WeatherSentry) to use for benchmarking purposes.
StormImpact: Provides proprietary weather and hurricane models that are customized with Georgia Power’s historical storm effects to predict infrastructure damage and outages for any scale event.
WeatherSentry: Robust platform that provides insights from proprietary weather prediction models on storm impacts using historical data to show damage, outages, and flooding. Among other capabilities, this system also provides data on traffic and evacuation routes to route restoration crews if necessary.

question:
Please provide all workpapers and load research data utilized to develop the following allocation factors utilized in Company Witness Evans’ CCOSS for Period I and Period II.  Provide in executable Excel format with all formulas and links intact:
class 12-CPs;
class NCPs;
kWh usage at generation or transmission level;
kWh usage at meter; and,
energy and demand loss factors by voltage level and rate schedule.

For each generating unit included in the Company’s Period I (12-months ending December 31, 2021), please provide the following in Excel format:
name of unit;
type of fuel(s) or source for power;
total and Georgia Power-owned (separately) nameplate capacity (MW);
total and Georgia Power (separately) gross investment at the end of the period;
total and Georgia Power (separately) depreciation reserve at the end of the period;
total and Georgia Power (separately) annual book depreciation expense;
total gross kWh produced during the period; and,
net (less station use) total and Georgia Power kWh (separately) produced during the period.

For each generating unit included in the Company’s Period II (12-months ending July 31, 2023), please provide the following in Excel format:
name of unit;
type of fuel(s) or source for power;
total and Georgia Power-owned (separately) nameplate capacity (MW);
total and Georgia Power (separately) gross investment at the end of the period;
total and Georgia Power (separately) depreciation reserve at the end of the period;
total and Georgia Power (separately) annual book depreciation expense;
total gross kWh produced during the period; and,
net (less station use) total and Georgia Power kWh (separately) produced during the period

For each Purchase Power Agreement (“PPA”) with costs included in the Company’s Period I (12-months ending December 31, 2021), provide the following in Excel format:
dedicated or contractual capacity to Georgia Power;
energy (MWh) provided to Georgia Power during test year;
capacity charges to Georgia Power; and,
energy charges to Georgia Power.

For each PPA with costs included in the Company’s Period II (12-months ending July 31, 2023), provide the following in Excel format:
dedicated or contractual capacity to Georgia Power;
energy (MWh) provided to Georgia Power during test year;
capacity charges to Georgia Power; and,
energy charges to Georgia Power.

For each month of 2021, please provide a bill frequency analysis utilizing the template provided below for each of the following Georgia Power Company rate schedules, separately:
Residential Rate R;
Residential Flat Bill;
Residential Rate TOU-REO;
Residential TOU-PEV;
Residential PPS;
Residential PBD; and,
Residential TOU-RD.
Template:
Provide in Excel format with formulas intact (preferably with each rate schedule in a separate file and each month in a separate tab).

For each month of 2021, please provide the following data for each Georgia Power Company Residential account:
rate schedule;
account number or a unique identification number (consistent across all months for each account);
bill date;
number of days in bill;
billed kWh;
maximum kW (preferably 60-minute interval, otherwise indicate time interval);
date of maximum kW; and,
time of maximum kW.
Provide in Microsoft Access or ASCII format (comma-delimited, or fixed field, and define field names).  If all requested data is not available, provide all data that is available and explain why certain data is not available.

For each month during the historical test year (1/1/21 through 12/31/21), please provide the following data for each net metering account under Rate RNR-10:
traditional (base) rate schedule;
indication if under RNR-Monthly Netting or RNR-Instantaneous Netting;
account number or unique identification number (consistent across all months for each account);
bill date;
number of days in bill;
delivered kWh from Georgia Power;
kWh received from customer;
net kWh;
Provide in Excel format, if possible, otherwise provide in Microsoft Access or ASCII format (comma-delimited or fixed field length and define field names).

Refer to the Company’s response to STF-WG-1-1 (e), which states “The Company is not deploying DERMS to increase the hosting capacity of the system”, and which refers to a brief whitepaper (TS Attachment D) which describes the capabilities and benefits of a DERMS. Refer also to the Company’s response to STF-WG-1-1 (i) and Attachment F, which describe potential complications of DER. Please describe:
a. How the Company currently maintains the capabilities and secures the benefits from the resources/devices that DERMS is anticipated to monitor/control (as described in TS Attachment D); and
b. How the Company currently manages the potential complications of DER (as described in response to SRF-WG-1-1 (i) and Attachment F;
c. Why the level of DER the Company forecasts adding 2022-2026 will require a DERMS to manage;
d. Any work the Company has performed in DER aggregation and distribution management and controls;
e. What efforts the Company has undertaken to begin adapting and integrating these efforts toward a centralized DERMS;
f. The alternatives to DERMS deployment the Company has evaluated.  Provide all associated analyses of DERMS and its alternatives the Company has completed;
g. Projected spending by year, by FERC account to implement DERMS 2023-2025, including the depreciation period the Company used to calculate revenue requirements in this case for each FERC account; and
h. Actual or projected spending by year, by FERC account to implement DERMS 2020-2022, including the depreciation period the Company used to calculate revenue requirements in this case for each FERC account.

Refer to the Company’s TS response to STF-REC-1-1 (A Customer Benefit Study of Distribution Investment). Page 8 states “REDACTED.” Pages 8 and 9 also include charts of historical SAIFI and SAIDI performance REDACTED. Refer also to TS Attachment A provided by the Company in response to STF-WG-1-1. Please add five columns to the MS Excel spreadsheet provided by the Company in TS Attachment A and indicate in those columns, for each circuit that is part of the REDACTED used to create the historical SAIDI and SAIFI charts on pages 8 and 9:
The GPC Region which manages the circuit;
The reliability rank of the circuit (for example, from 1 to 1xx, with 1 being least reliable) within the respective GPC Region (as described in the TS response STF-REC-1-1, page 7);
A list of the Packages GPC has applied or proposes to apply to the circuit;
The estimated cost of the Packages GPC estimates will be applied to the circuit through December 31, 2022 (such that the amounts for Packages completed through 2022 approximates $REDACTED indicated to be spent through 2022 in the response to TS Attachment STF-WG-1-18); and
The amounts for Packages to be applied after December 31, 2022 (such that the total approximates $REDACTED in capital spending per STF-REC-1-1, page (i)).

Refer to the Customer Benefits Study provided by the Company in response to STF-L&A-5-38 in Docket No. 42516, Table 1.2 on pages 2-3, and the eight “Packages” listed.  For each of the eight Packages listed, please provide:
Actual spending by FERC Account in 2020, along with the depreciation period for each;
Actual spending by FERC Account in 2021, along with the depreciation period for each;
Projected spending by FERC Account 2022; along with the depreciation period for each.

Refer to the November 9, 2022 hearing transcript page 1838, line 13 to page 1839, line 25.  For each substation equipment type, including i) power transformers; ii) circuit breakers; iii) relays; iv) switches; v) load-tap changers; vi) bushings; and vii) CTs, please provide:
The Iowa Survivor Curve (indicating years in consistent units along the horizontal, or “x” axis) employed by the Company’s depreciation expert to determine the depreciation rate for the substation equipment type;
The historical failure rate data on which the Company’s depreciation expert relied when determining which Iowa Survivor Curve to select for the substation equipment type;
The expected useful life (in years) the Company employs for the substation equipment type when selecting equipment of that type for replacement as part of its transmission investment plan;
The historical failure rate data on which the Company relied when determining the expected useful life (in years) for the substation equipment type.  If historical failure rate data on which the Company relied is different than that provided to the Company’s depreciation expert, please explain why.
